<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · South Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Kuninda Kingdom</h1><p class="st-meta">200 BCE – 300 CE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Himalayan kingdom of the upper Sutlej and Yamuna, known from its fine silver coins and mentioned by Ptolemy.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Kunindas, or Kulindas, appear in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahabharata">Mahabharata</a> as a mountain people, and Ptolemy places a land called Kulindrine near the sources of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beas">Beas</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sutlej-river">Sutlej</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yamuna">Yamuna</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganges-plain">Ganges</a>. They emerged as a state in the foothills of modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/himachal-pradesh">Himachal Pradesh</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uttarakhand">Uttarakhand</a> around the 2nd century BCE, after the fall of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/maurya-empire">Maurya Empire</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The kingdom is known mainly from coins. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> coins of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amoghabhuti">Amoghabhuti</a>, modelled on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indo-greek-kingdom">Indo-Greek</a> drachms, carry legends in both Brahmi and Kharoshthi with a deer and a goddess, possibly <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lakshmi">Lakshmi</a>. Later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> coins show <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiva">Shiva</a> as Chatreshvara, lord of the parasol, suggesting a shift to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shaivism">Shaiva</a> worship. The economy probably depended on trade in forest and mountain products with the plains.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Kuninda coinage seems to have stopped during the expansion of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kushan-empire">Kushan Empire</a> and resumed later. By the 4th century the Kunindas disappear from the record, possibly absorbed by the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/yaudheya-republic">Yaudheya republic</a> or by the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/gupta-empire">Gupta Empire</a>.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/kuninda-kingdom.jpg" alt="Kuninda Kingdom" width="320" height="160"><figcaption>Image: World Imaging · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:KunindaCoin.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>200 BCE – 300 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Amoghabhuti</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Hinduism, Shaivism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Prakrit</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>India</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Kunindas, Kulindas</dd></dl></aside></div>

